00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.099
Leah: Let’s start by reviewing
what we have already discussed.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.240
• How DRY code helps avoid repetition.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.680
• How KISS principles help
to keep code maintainable.

00:00:11.259 --> 00:00:13.829
• How coding conventions keep code readable.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.459
• How SOLID principles help us
produce modular accessible code.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.730
• How testing allows us to identify bugs.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.600
• How debugging allows us
to rectify these mistakes.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:30.400
Jack: So, what if a program crashes
when an error or bug occurs, how can

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.460
we prepare our code to deal with this?

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.329
We can use ‘robust programming principles’
to ensure that we can pre-empt and

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.000
deal with errors appropriately.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.290
‘Robust coding and programming’
focus on handling invalid

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.370
inputs, actions, and errors.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.690
It utilises specific code to
handle these by displaying accurate

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.610
and unambiguous error messages.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.300
This way, it can deal with specific
errors in a way that prevents termination

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.490
of the program, or it can provide error
messages which provide clear information

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:04.810
to the user or programmer on what caused
the error so that they can debug it.

00:01:06.059 --> 00:01:09.170
Leah: With robust programming
you must bear in mind 4 basic

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.740
things when writing your code.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.570
Let’s start with Paranoia.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.550
Assume that users of your code
aim to break it, and that it can

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.710
fail or run incorrectly by itself.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.289
Therefore, you should write error
handling code accordingly and don’t trust

00:01:24.309 --> 00:01:26.410
anything you haven’t generated yourself.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.829
Next let’s look at Stupidity.

00:01:29.529 --> 00:01:34.399
Write the error messages assuming that
the caller or user is stupid and unable

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.000
to read manual pages of documentation.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.780
This way, the error messages should be
self-explanatory so other users of the

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.199
code [and yourself after time has passed!]
will be able to understand the problem.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.670
Jack: Keep Dangerous
Implements away from users.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.270
Anything that your routines expect
to remain consistent across calls are

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.279
known as ‘dangerous implements’ and
should be kept inaccessible to users.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.640
They may [accidently or even deliberately]
if accessed, modify, and affect the

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:07.490
way the program runs in ways that
they had not intended [side effects].

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.070
This also makes the code more modular.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:13.179
And finally, don’t think it can't happen.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.580
Never assume that it is impossible that
certain bugs or errors can occur so write

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.440
the error handling code accordingly.

00:02:21.299 --> 00:02:25.539
In the worst case scenario, your program
should at least contain code for flagging

00:02:25.539 --> 00:02:29.850
an error message in seemingly impossible
cases, even if it cannot fix the issue.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:30.774
If you anticipate

00:02:30.774 --> 00:02:40.149
Leah: the problems which may occur, you
can use robust programming to secure your

00:02:40.149 --> 00:02:41.189
code against the occurrence of errors,
and respond appropriately to either keep

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.420
the program running, or to provide the
user with the exact nature of the error.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.100
This is done through employing
appropriate defensive programming

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:52.820
and exception handling which you will
learn more about in this session.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:54.940
Jack: Let’s have a look
at this in more detail.

